parsed.P_prime:
Three lines

\[
\begin{aligned}
L_1: &\ \vec{r}=\lambda(\hat{i}+\hat{j}),\ \lambda\in\mathbb{R},\\
L_2: &\ \vec{r}=\hat{k}+\mu\hat{j},\ \mu\in\mathbb{R},\\
L_3: &\ \vec{r}=\hat{i}+\hat{j}+\nu\hat{k},\ \nu\in\mathbb{R}
\end{aligned}
\]

are given. For which point(s) \(Q\) on \(L_2\) can we find a point \(P\) on \(L_1\) and a point \(R\) on \(L_3\) so that \(P,Q,R\) are collinear?

For a real number \(\alpha\), if the system  
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & \alpha & \alpha^2\\
\alpha & 1 & \alpha\\
1+\alpha^2 & 2\alpha & 2\alpha^2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix}
\]
has infinitely many solutions, then what is the value of \(1+\alpha+\alpha^2\)?

